# 🏭 Factory Method vs. Abstract Factory Pattern

## 📊 Summary Comparison

| Feature                     | Factory Method Pattern                                        | Abstract Factory Pattern                                               |
| :-------------------------- | :------------------------------------------------------------ | :--------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Primary Intent**          | Creates **one product**; subclasses decide exact class        | Creates **families of related products** without specifying classes    |
| **Core Mechanism**          | **Inheritance** (Subclasses override factory method)          | **Composition / Object Delegation** (Factory injected into client)     |
| **Method Signature**        | Single creation method (e.g. `createButton()`)                | Multiple creation methods per family (`createButton()`, `createCheckbox()`) |
| **Product Scope**           | Single product hierarchy (`Button`)                           | Multiple product hierarchies (`Button`, `Checkbox`, `TextField`)       |
| **Coupling**                | Tied to Creator inheritance hierarchy                         | Decoupled via interface; factory object passed into client             |
| **Consistency Enforcement** | N/A (creates one object at a time)                            | **Guaranteed**: Products from the same family are always paired together|
| **Adding New Product Types**| Simple: Add a new subclass of Creator                         | Hard: Requires changing `GUIFactory` interface and all implementations  |
| **Adding New Variants**     | Easy: Add a new Creator subclass returning the new Product    | Easy: Add a new Concrete Factory implementing `GUIFactory`             |

